\section{Literature Review}
% ============================================================

Research in financial artificial intelligence has explored the use of natural language processing, sentiment analysis, machine learning, deep learning, and large language models for financial applications.

\begin{enumerate}

    \item
    \textbf{Araci (2019)} proposed FinBERT, a BERT-based language model adapted for financial sentiment analysis. The work demonstrated that domain-specific language models can improve the interpretation of financial text compared with general-purpose language models. This work provides the basis for applying financial sentiment analysis to news and financial documents in the proposed system.

    \item
    \textbf{FinGPT research} explored the use of open-source large language models for financial applications. The approach demonstrates the potential of large language models for financial information processing, analysis, and question answering. The proposed project uses similar concepts through a locally deployable language model and retrieval-augmented generation.

    \item
    Traditional machine learning and deep learning approaches have also been extensively explored for stock market prediction. Models such as Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks can learn patterns from historical market data and technical indicators. However, stock prices are highly noisy and influenced by multiple external factors, making exact price prediction difficult.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Research Gap}

Existing research frequently focuses on individual components such as stock prediction, financial sentiment analysis, or financial language models. A practical limitation is the lack of an integrated educational platform that combines real-time market data, technical indicators, financial sentiment, machine learning predictions, explainable AI analysis, backtesting, portfolio risk analysis, and paper trading in a single system.

The proposed project attempts to address this gap by integrating these components into a unified platform. Instead of treating prediction as an isolated task, the system will combine multiple information sources and provide probabilistic analysis along with historical backtesting and paper trading evaluation.

% ============================================================
\section{Objectives}
% ============================================================

The major objectives of the proposed project are:

\begin{enumerate}

    \item To develop a web-based platform for real-time and historical stock market analysis.

    \item To collect and process market data including OHLCV (Open, High, Low, Close, Volume) information and real-time price updates.

    \item To implement technical analysis indicators such as SMA, EMA, RSI, MACD, Bollinger Bands, ATR, ADX, and VWAP.

    \item To collect financial news and perform financial sentiment analysis using domain-specific natural language processing models such as FinBERT.

    \item To develop machine learning models for estimating the probability of upward or downward stock movement.

    \item To compare different prediction approaches including traditional machine learning and deep learning models.

    \item To develop an AI-based financial assistant capable of explaining stock-related information using retrieved market data, news, sentiment, and technical indicators.

    \item To implement a paper trading environment using virtual capital.

    \item To implement backtesting functionality for evaluating trading strategies using historical data.

    \item To provide portfolio performance and risk metrics such as return, volatility, Sharpe ratio, and maximum drawdown.

\end{enumerate}

