[L601] \subsection{Technical Feasibility}

[L603] The project is technically feasible because the required technologies are available as open-source frameworks, libraries, or free-tier services. JavaScript/TypeScript, Python, PostgreSQL, Redis, Docker, Scikit-learn, PyTorch, Hugging Face Transformers, and open-source language models can be used to implement the system.

[L605] Market data and news will be collected through available APIs and historical data sources. The architecture will be designed so that individual data providers can be replaced if their free-tier limitations or availability change.

[L607] \subsection{Economic Feasibility}

[L609] The initial development version is designed to minimize infrastructure cost by using open-source software, local machine learning models, local LLM inference through Ollama, PostgreSQL, Redis, and available free-tier APIs.

[L611] The project does not require real-money trading infrastructure because the initial implementation uses paper trading with virtual capital. Therefore, the prototype can be developed with minimal financial expenditure.

[L613] \subsection{Operational Feasibility}

[L615] The proposed system is designed as a web application with an intuitive dashboard. Users will be able to search for stocks, inspect charts, view technical indicators, read financial news, examine sentiment, view model predictions, and perform paper trades.

[L617] The platform will be primarily intended for educational, analytical, and research purposes. Predictions will be presented as model-generated probabilities rather than guaranteed future prices.

